  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia funds the Director of the Solomon Islands Maritime Authority (SIMA), responsible for maritime safety, security and pollution prevention. SIMA ensures compliance of ships and ports with security laws. In 2024, out of 777 calls of 320 foreign-flagged ships in Solomon Islands&#x2019; ports, SIMA verified 9 ships of higher security risk (compared to 3 ships over 2021-2023). SIMA developed security guidelines while fostering cooperation with border security agencies and private stakeholders members of the Maritime Security Committee, and contributed to the development of the National Maritime Security Policy. SIMA supports maritime boundaries-related activities associated with the Solomon Islands Government &#x2018;Ocean12&#x2019; initiative &#x2013; a national, inter-ministerial committee established in 2016 that oversees and implements initiatives to protect, manage and properly govern the ocean. SIMA&#x2019;s contribution builds on the mentoring and training of three officers (1 M; 2 F) in security and audit who conduct security audits of international ports and ships, and assess ship security risk.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Maritime security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia provides technical assistance support to the Solomon Islands Maritime Authority (SIMA), responsible for international, regional, and national maritime obligations under the SIMA Act 2018 on behalf of the Solomon Islands Govern-ment. SIMA has the mandate on maritime security under the international Con-vention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS Convention), the International Ship and Port Security Code (ISPS) and the Maritime Safety Administration (Ship and Port Security) Regulations 2011. Director SIMA represents the Designated Author-ity (DA), as per the above laws, and is the Chair of the national Maritime Security Committee (MSC) established by the 2011 Regulations. In 2023, SIMA developed MSC-approved security guidelines, and new measures were adopted to identify and approve sufferance ports (used by international vessels engaged in logging and mining). SIMA progressed development of its vessel tracking system by up-grading its radiocommunication system, Automatic Identification System (AIS) and Long-Range Identification and Tracking System (LRIT).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s Solomon Islands Infrastructure Program (SIIP) is supporting the Ministry of Finance and Treasury and the Ministry of National Planning and Development Cooperation to develop an Infrastructure Asset Management Policy (IAMP). The policy will improve the oversight of national public infrastructure and support evidence-based prioritisation of assets for maintenance funding. In FY24/25, SIIP helped develop the policy document and facilitated training in asset management for Government officials, including through courses in Australia (October 2024 and May 2025), which were well received.  
&#x200d;
 As part of this work, SIIP is supporting Ministry of Infrastructure Development (MID) to develop a Strategic Asset Management Plan to improve management of transport infrastructure. MID is the first Government agency to develop such a plan under the IAMP and will use it to inform its annual budgeting and investment decisions relating to maintenance and upgrades of its infrastructure assets.  
&#x200d;
In FY24/25, SIIP supported MID to procure asset management software, RoadRoid, and delivered training in its use. SIIP also provided training to 19 MID officers in the use of SITAMS software, which contains infrastructure asset data and assists with prioritisation of assets for maintenance funding, enabling MID to fully operationalise the software for the first time.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In mid-2023, the Permanent Secretary of the Ministry of National Planning and Development Coordination (MNPDC) requested SIIP support to review and up-date the Solomon Islands Priority Infrastructure Investment Pipeline (SINIPP), originally produced in 2021. SIIP supported MNPDC to update and restructure the pipeline, adopting a place-based infrastructure planning approach. Senior MNPDC staff provided oversight and guidance throughout and successfully steered the document through Cabinet endorsement in December 2023. The pipeline was launched in February 2024, with Caretaker Prime Minister Manasseh Sogavare noting the SINIPP 2023 identifies the most significant infrastructure programs for decades to come, consolidated into a single document.&#x201d; At MNPDC&#x2019;s request SIIP is providing further support to socialise the pipeline nation-ally and progress next stages of its implementation, which shows the policy out-come has been sustained and will continue.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Solomon Islands Infrastructure Program (SIIP) supported fifteen economic and social infrastructure projects during FY24-25 aligned to G20 principles, Australian Standards, and Australian Government policies ensuring quality, inclusivity, safety, social and environmental safeguarding, climate change and disaster resilience and local content opportunities. All SIIP designs meet Tropical Cyclone Category 4 requirements and New Zealand earthquake standard. SIIP infrastructure is future-proofed to be resilient to disaster and changes in climate. 
&#x200d;
The projects include: Buala Market, Seghe Market, Malu&#x2019;u Market, Naha Birthing and Urban Health Centre, Malaita South Road design, Noro Port design, Buala Wharf design, Kirakira Market (scoping), Tenaru Market (scoping), Passport Office Refurbishment, Telekom Towers, South Road Emergency Works, Taro Airfield Terminal, Seghe Airfield Terminal</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Infrastructure</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Solomon Islands Infrastructure Program (SIIP) supported ten economic and social infrastructure projects aligned to G20 principles, Australian Standards, and Australian Government policies ensuring quality, inclusivity, safety, social and en-vironmental safeguarding, climate change and disaster resilience (CCDR), and lo-cal content opportunities. The Tulagi Market Refurbishment included a perimeter fence to enhance the safety of vendors, who are mostly women, and a ramp to provide access for people with disabilities. All SIIP designs meet Tropical Cyclone Category 4 requirements and New Zealand earthquake standard. SIIP infrastruc-ture is future proofed to be resilient to disaster and changes in climate. The Naha Birthing and Urban Health Centre&#x2019;s orientation will provide better solar control and enables access to cooling easterly breezes, and the building materials will in-crease thermal performance and reduce solar heat gain. SIIP&#x2019;s Local Content Strategy, which is applied across all projects, identifies opportunities to maximise benefits for local construction companies and suppliers.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Solomon Islands Infrastructure Program (SIIP), four people with disabilities (2 men, 2 women) were supported to complete the Australian-accredited CPPACC4005 &#x2013; Conduct Building Access Audits certification, enabling them to carry out &#x2018;access auditing&#x2019; with building inspectors. This will help to increase the inclusive application of Solomon Islands&#x2019; forthcoming National Building Code. Adjustments were made to the course providing additional time for the students to complete it so they could meet their learning goals.  
&#x200d;
The training complements SIIP&#x2019;s support to the National Disability Forum Solomon Islands (NDFSI), specifically the development of a business plan to help establish income-earning activities such as delivering accessibility audits. SIIP will explore contracting NDFSI to conduct accessibility audits of completed infrastructure projects in the future. 
&#x200d;
19 women completed Certificate II in Construction from Australia Pacific Training Coalition in October 2024. The women completed a course in basic solar system design, installation and maintenance through the Solomon Islands National University (SINU). They challenged stereotypes about the role of women in construction industry, leading the way for more women to pparticipate in this male-dominated sector.</narrative>
    </description>
